## Why This Is Asked
Tests expertise in fully dynamic Li Chao trees, offline lifetime decomposition, and practical handling of duplicates in production settings.

## Key Concepts
- **Segment tree over time** for lifetime interval decomposition
- **Per-node Li Chao trees** for minimum query aggregation
- **Path-query traversal** across O(log N) nodes
- Memory-time trade-offs for handling large N and duplicates

## Code Example
```javascript
class LiChao {
  insert(line) { /* insert line into this node's Li Chao tree */ }
  query(x) { /* return minimum y at x across stored lines */ }
}

class DynamicLineSet {
  constructor(N) {
    this.segmentTree = new SegmentTree(N);
  }

  addLine(m, b, id, insertTime) {
    const interval = [insertTime, Infinity];
    this.segmentTree.addInterval(interval, { m, b, id });
  }

  removeLine(id, removeTime) {
    // Update the line's interval end time in the segment tree
    this.segmentTree.closeInterval(id, removeTime);
  }

  queryMin(x, queryTime) {
    return this.segmentTree.queryPath(queryTime)
      .reduce((min, lichao) => Math.min(min, lichao.query(x)), Infinity);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| addLine | O(log N) | O(log N) per line |
| removeLine | O(log N) | - |
| queryMin | O(log² N) | - |

## Edge Cases
- **Identical slopes**: Stored separately with unique ids; Li Chao tree handles min correctly
- **Immediate removal**: Assigned to leaf node only
- **Large N**: Memory scales with O(N log N) total line-node assignments

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of practical queue augmentations and min-tracking with guaranteed O(1) per operation.

## Key Concepts
- Min-tracking with stack-based queues
- Amortized analysis of transfers between stacks
- Edge-case handling for empty states and duplicates

## Code Example
```javascript
class MinQueue {
  constructor(){ this.inbox = []; this.outbox = [] }
  _push(stack, val){ const m = stack.length ? Math.min(val, stack[stack.length - 1].min) : val; stack.push({ val, min: m }) }
  enqueue(x){ this._push(this.inbox, x) }
  dequeue(){ if (!this.outbox.length){ while (this.inbox.length){ const el = this.inbox.pop(); const m = this.outbox.length ? Math.min(el.val, this.outbox[this.outbox.length - 1].min) : el.val; this.outbox.push({ val: el.val, min: m }) } }
    return this.outbox.pop().val }
  getMin(){ const mins = []; if (this.inbox.length) mins.push(this.inbox[this.inbox.length - 1].min); if (this.outbox.length) mins.push(this.outbox[this.outbox.length - 1].min); return mins.length ? Math.min(...mins) : undefined }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine a hashmap with a priority structure to track a dynamic mode under duplicates, while keeping updates O(log n) on average. It also checks handling of stale entries and edge cases (removals of non-existent values).

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for value-to-count to enable O(1) updates
- Max-heap keyed by (count, -value) to prioritize higher counts and break ties toward smaller values
- Lazy deletion of stale heap entries to keep updates efficient
- Edge cases: removing non-existent values, empty multiset

## Code Example
```javascript
class ModeMultiset {
  constructor() {
    this.cnt = new Map();
    this.heap = []; // max-heap of [count, -value]
  }
  _heapPush(item) { /* standard binary-heap insert with comparator by count desc, value asc */ }
  _heapPop() { /* standard binary-heap extract-max */ }
  addValue(x) {
    const c = (this.cnt.get(x) || 0) + 1;
    this.cnt.set(x, c);
    this._heapPush([c, -x]);
  }
  removeValue(x) {
    const c = this.cnt.get(x);
    if (!c) return;
    const nc = c - 1;
    if (nc > 0) this.cnt.set(x, nc);
    else this.cnt.delete(x);
    if (nc > 0) this._heapPush([nc, -x]);
  }
  getMode() {
    while (this.heap.length) {
      const [c, neg] = this.heap[0];
      const v = -neg;
      if ((this.cnt.get(v) || 0) === c) return v;
      this._heapPop();
    }
    return null;
  }
}
```

